    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">02</span><div class="num">Chapter Two</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="ready">Start before you feel ready</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Start your email list on day one, even with zero subscribers. The list is how you build the audience you keep — waiting until you "have an audience" gets the order exactly backwards.</p>
    <p class="reveal">The empty room feels like a reason to wait. It isn't. The first subscriber is harder to get than the thousandth, so the sooner you start, the sooner the compounding begins. <strong>A list of twelve people you started this month beats a list of zero you'll start "once you're bigger" — because the second one never actually starts.</strong></p>

    <div class="photo-quote reveal">
      <div class="photo dark" style="position:absolute;inset:0;background-image:url(/images/library/portrait-woman-side-desk.jpg);background-size:cover;background-position:center"></div>
      <div class="veil"></div>
      <div class="inner"><p>The best time to start your list was your first day online. The second best time is today.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✕</span> Common Mistake</span><p>Waiting to start your email list "until you have an audience." Backwards. The list <em>is</em> how you build the audience you keep. Start on day one, even if day one is quiet — quiet is where every real list begins.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">03</span><div class="num">Chapter Three</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="magnet">What a lead magnet actually needs to do</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">A lead magnet is a small free resource someone receives in exchange for their email. Its only job is to solve one narrow problem completely — enough that the person is glad they gave you their address.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Beginners over-build this. A lead magnet doesn't need to be a 40-page ebook; that's often worse. <strong>One sharp, complete solution to one small problem beats a sprawling one that solves nothing fully.</strong> The person should finish it thinking "that was genuinely useful" — because that first impression is what makes them open email number two.</p>

    <div class="framework reveal">
      <span class="tag"><span>✦</span> The Lead Magnet Test</span>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="node"><div class="h">One Problem</div><div class="t">Narrow enough to solve completely in a page or two.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">One Win</div><div class="t">They finish it with a result, not just information.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">One Reason to Stay</div><div class="t">Good enough that email two gets opened.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>The best lead magnet is often a piece of the thing you already sell or teach — a single template, one checklist, the first step of a bigger system. It solves a real problem <em>and</em> shows exactly what more you offer.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">04</span><div class="num">Chapter Four</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="welcome">The welcome sequence that does the work</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">A welcome sequence is a short series of automated emails a new subscriber receives after signing up. It introduces you, delivers on the promise that got them in, and turns a stranger into someone who trusts you — while you sleep.</p>
    <p class="reveal">This is the highest-leverage writing in your whole business. You write it once, and every future subscriber receives it automatically, in their most engaged moment — right after they raised their hand. <strong>A good welcome sequence is the difference between a list that opens your emails and one that forgot who you are.</strong></p>

    <div class="detail reveal">
      <div class="photo mask img" style="background-image:url(/images/library/detail-hands-typing-cinematic.jpg);background-size:cover;background-position:center"></div>
      <div class="say"><div class="k">Why it matters most</div><p>The welcome sequence reaches people at peak attention — the moment they just subscribed. No email you send later will ever be opened as reliably. Write it like it's the only one that counts, because it nearly is.</p></div>
    </div>
